+        for key, value in report.items()
+        if key.startswith("G") and key[1:2].isdigit()
+    )
+    return report
+
+
+if __name__ == "__main__":
+    print(json.dumps(selftest(), indent=2, sort_keys=True))

codex
The first implementation is in place. It uses a balanced bipartite tree as the certificate source, then applies only exact structure-preserving integer encodings; the verifier sees neither the planted tree nor the planted answer. The full selftest is now running, including 200,000 structure-aware guesses and eight exact meet-in-the-middle reference solves, so this pass is also measuring the Track B cost rather than estimating it from search-space size.
codex
The paper’s native decision/search objects are clear: bin assignments and schedules, with exact integer loads. Its own upper bounds are pseudopolynomial dynamic programs—so a planted family cannot honestly claim Track A merely from the paper’s worst-case lower bounds. I’m evaluating a Track B construction where the mechanical DP is explicitly measured and the intended route is a short invariant-based decoding.
