index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..75c9770205ef4007a66069ac139f480ea256b519
--- /dev/null
+++ b/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,136 @@
+# Verified generator for arXiv:2603.12999
+
+| Profile field | Value |
+|---|---|
+| Track | **B — no-tool compression** |
+| Native domain | `optimization` |
+| Object regime | `finite_discrete` |
+| Computational core | `subset_sum` |
+| Certificate form | `integer_tuple` (two sets of item indices) |
+| Intended intuition | `change of variables`: remove equal-width decimal-block masks |
+| Domain essentiality | `native` |
+| Reduction | none |
+
+This directory turns Bringmann, Dürr, and Węgrzycki’s [*Tight (S)ETH-based Lower Bounds for Pseudopolynomial Algorithms for Bin Packing and Multi-Machine Scheduling*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.12999) into a witness task in the paper’s native objects. The solver receives a set of near-equal positive integer sizes and two equal-capacity bins. It must partition all item indices into two equal-cardinality sets whose exact integer sums both equal the capacity. Verification is just range/disjointness checking and exact addition; it never reads `inst["answer"]`.
+
+## Why this is faithful, generatable, and Track B
+
+Appendix A defines Bounded (k)-way Partition. Observation 3.4 proves that its equal-load parts have equal cardinality, Lemma 8.14 identifies the one-group version with Bounded (k)-way Partition, and Theorem 8.1 makes this native problem parameter-preserving equivalent to (k)-Bin Packing and (P_k||C_{\max}). Section 2.1 encodes consistency using carry-free blocks inside ordinary integers; this generator uses that same mathematical device without replacing the integers by a graph in the rendered problem.
+
+Generation is inverse. It first chooses two colour classes of equal size, constructs a connected tree using only cross-class edges, turns its edge-incidence columns into integer blocks, adds an independently sampled common mask to every column, permutes columns and items, and adds a common high offset. Every edge contributes once to each planted bin, so the certificate is known before the integers exist. The offset makes the values satisfy the paper’s exact bounded interval. No generated instance is solved.
+
+Theorem 1.1 is a worst-case ETH lower bound, not an average-case theorem for this distribution, so it does not justify Track A. The paper also explicitly gives the easy mechanisms: Section 1.1 records the (O(nT)) subset-sum dynamic program, a newer \(\widetilde O(n+T)\) algorithm, and exponential-in-(n) exact algorithms; Section 9 stresses the known (O((2-\varepsilon_k)^n)) breakthrough for fixed (k). Lemma 8.14 also notes that the bounded regime (W<n^{10}) is polynomial-time. The generator uses (W\gg n^{10}), but openly remains algorithmic.
+
+The measured Track B reference is exact balanced meet-in-the-middle subset sum, running in (O(2^{n/2})) time and space. At shipping (n=38), eight seeds averaged 668,750 visited states, 2,295,171 counted operations, and 4.158242 seconds (maximum 5.400438 seconds), solving 8/8. That is routine for software and out of reach by hand. The compact route groups the decimal strings into equal-width blocks, removes each column’s common baseline, and propagates the resulting two-endpoint constraints. Its conservative budget is 187 exact operations.
+
+## Worked demo
+
+The `demo` preset with seed 3 renders in full as follows:
+
+```text
+Bounded two-way integer partition
+
+You are given a set of distinct positive integer item sizes and two
+identical bins.  A partition assigns every item to exactly one bin.
+The load of a bin is the exact sum of its assigned item sizes.
+
+There are 6 items, indexed 0 through 5.  Each bin has capacity
+T = 1813990951316101610.  The listed sizes have total 2T.  They also lie
+in the inclusive interval [W*(1-1/n^10), W] with W = 604663650505030603.
+Consequently a valid equal-load partition has exactly n/2 items per bin;
+your answer must explicitly put exactly 3 indices in each bin.
+Bins are labelled 0 and 1, but swapping the two whole bins is accepted.
+Indices within each bin must be strictly increasing.  Repeats and
+omissions are not allowed, and all arithmetic is over ordinary decimal integers.
+
+ITEM SIZES (0-based index: decimal size):
+0: 604663650505030503
+1: 604663650406040603
+2: 604663650405040504
+3: 604663650406030503
+4: 604663650405030504
+5: 604663650505030603
+
+Return a JSON array [bin_0, bin_1], where each bin is a JSON array
+of exactly 3 distinct 0-based integer indices.  Together the
+two arrays must contain every index 0 through 5 exactly once.
+Give your final answer inside <answer></answer> tags, in that exact format.
+Example syntax only: <answer>[[0,2,5],[1,3,4]]</answer>
+Output nothing else inside the tags.
+```
+
+The answer is `[[2,3,5],[0,1,4]]`. `verify(inst, inst["answer"])` returns `(True, "ok")`. Exchanging index 2 with index 0 returns `(False, "capacity mismatch: the two exact loads are not both T")`. A person can solve this demo on paper: split the last ten digits into five two-digit blocks, remove the common value in each column, and follow the five resulting pair constraints.
+
+## Difficulty presets
+
+| Preset | Items `n` | Decimal block width | Balanced candidates | Status |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| demo | 6 | 2 | 20 | hand illustration; not shipped |
+| easy | 38 | 3 | 35,345,263,800 | **intended shipping preset; local gates pass** |
+| medium | 40 | 4 | 137,846,528,820 | available if the oracle solves easy |
+| hard | 42 | 5 | 538,257,874,440 | available if the oracle solves medium |
+
+`escalate()` raises both `n` and the coefficient/block width, so the balanced-subset haystack and the paper’s pseudopolynomial parameter grow together. It reports `cap_bound` only before the 256-index certificate limit would be crossed.
+
+## Gate results
+
+| Gate | Result | Measured evidence |
+|---|---|---|
+| G1 | pass | 12/12 preset/seed witnesses verified; every answer JSON-round-tripped |
+| G2 | pass | drop, swap, duplicate, empty, and out-of-range rejected with 5 distinct reasons |
+| G3 | pass | tagged JSON recovered from prose and Markdown fences |
+| G4 | pass | 0/200,000 structured guesses; candidate space 35,345,263,800 |
+| G5 | pass | 2 structural solutions; exact density `5.6584667505013784e-11`; reference cost 668,750 states / 2,295,171 operations / 4.158242 s average |
+| G6 | pass | five attacks at 0/8; exact reference algorithm at 8/8 |
+| G7 | pass | doubling `n` 38→76 raises candidate entropy 35.04→72.55 bits and still verifies |
+| G8 | pass | 60/60 relabellings invariant and valid; 20/20 unrelated keys distinct |
+| G9(c) | pass | 109 characters, 28 estimated tokens, 38 atoms, 187 intended operations |
+
+The failing G6 panel contains largest-half magnitude outliers, balanced largest-processing-time-first greedy, sorted alternation, a low-block parity ansatz, and 256 random restarts. Every item is produced by the same masked incidence law; there is no specially distributed planted item.
+
+## Oracle loop and G9 diagnostic
+
+The required script-owned OpenRouter run is currently **infrastructure-blocked, not hardened**. Every redraw returned HTTP 403 `Key limit exceeded (total limit)`, after which `harden.py` correctly exited rather than treating an API error as a solver failure. The error-only transcript is preserved, but it is not valid hardness evidence and this result is not ready for submission until the account limit is restored and all three arms are rerun.
+
+| Preset | Model | Seed | Scored? | Outcome |
+|---|---|---:|---|---|
+| easy | Gemini 3.1 Pro | 286836211 | no | HTTP 403 key limit |
+| easy | GPT-5.6 Terra | 276328457 | no | HTTP 403 key limit |
+| easy | Gemini 3.1 Pro | 278575903 | no | HTTP 403 key limit |
+| easy | GPT-5.6 Terra | 1161100860 | no | HTTP 403 key limit |
+
+| G9 arm | Solved / scored attempts | Verdict |
+|---|---:|---|
+| bare | 0 / 0 | pending infrastructure recovery |
+| structural hint | 0 / 0 | pending infrastructure recovery |
+| placebo hint | 0 / 0 | pending infrastructure recovery |
+
+No hinted-minus-placebo conclusion is possible from zero scored calls. The local suitability measurements remain 109 characters, 28 estimated tokens, 38 atomic indices, and 187 intended exact operations.
+
+## Use
+
+```python
+from gen_2603_12999 import DIFFICULTY, make_instance, parse_answer, render, verify
+import json
+
+inst = make_instance(seed=42, **DIFFICULTY["easy"])
+prompt = render(inst)
+candidate = parse_answer("<answer>" + json.dumps(inst["answer"]) + "</answer>")
+assert verify(inst, candidate) == (True, "ok")
+```
+
+From the repository root, after a successful bare hardening run, emit deterministic samples with:
+
+```bash
+bash scripts/emit.sh 2603.12999 20
+```
+
+## Caveats
+
+- This is a promised Track B subfamily. The paper proves no average-case hardness for it, and its worst-case ETH theorem must not be read as such evidence.
+- The exact density and the 0/200,000 experiment use the declared prior: a uniformly sampled balanced bipartition with all obvious cardinality constraints enforced. They are not the success probability of a solver that recognizes decimal blocks.
+- Removing the common block mask exposes a tree and makes the family linear-time. That is intentional: recognizing this change of variables is the benchmarked insight.
+- The standard pseudopolynomial dynamic program is wholly impractical because `T` has hundreds of digits; the measured practical reference is meet-in-the-middle. A general LLL implementation was not available under the standard-library-only dependency rule and was not run. Low-density lattice reduction may be effective and remains the main untested specialist attack.
+- The canonical key fully ignores item ordering, digit-column ordering, common high translation, bin exchange, and tree-vertex labels by canonicalizing the decoded unlabelled tree. Other arithmetic equivalences are not canonicalized.
+- `gvlib` is imported opportunistically as required by the repository layout, but this integer-only certificate needs none of its helpers and remains standard-library-only when it is absent.
+- The OpenRouter failure is a real completion blocker. The bare and two G9 files currently contain only script-produced HTTP-error rows; all three must be overwritten by successful `harden.py` runs after the key limit is fixed, never hand-edited or treated as solver failures.
