-This directory turns Bringmann, Dürr, and Węgrzycki’s [*Tight (S)ETH-based Lower Bounds for Pseudopolynomial Algorithms for Bin Packing and Multi-Machine Scheduling*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.12999) into a witness task in the paper’s native objects. The solver receives a set of near-equal positive integer sizes and two equal-capacity bins. It must partition all item indices into two equal-cardinality sets whose exact integer sums both equal the capacity. Verification is just range/disjointness checking and exact addition; it never reads `inst["answer"]`.
-
-## Why this is faithful, generatable, and Track B
+This module turns Bringmann, Dürr, and Węgrzycki’s [*Tight (S)ETH-based Lower Bounds for Pseudopolynomial Algorithms for Bin Packing and Multi-Machine Scheduling*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.12999) into an exact witness problem in the paper’s own objects. The solver receives groups of two near-equal positive integers. It chooses one integer from every group for bin A, with the other going to bin B, so that both exact loads equal the displayed target. Checking a witness is just shape checking and integer addition; `verify` never reads `inst["answer"]`.
 
-Appendix A defines Bounded (k)-way Partition. Observation 3.4 proves that its equal-load parts have equal cardinality, Lemma 8.14 identifies the one-group version with Bounded (k)-way Partition, and Theorem 8.1 makes this native problem parameter-preserving equivalent to (k)-Bin Packing and (P_k||C_{\max}). Section 2.1 encodes consistency using carry-free blocks inside ordinary integers; this generator uses that same mathematical device without replacing the integers by a graph in the rendered problem.
+## Why this is generatable and Track B
 
-Generation is inverse. It first chooses two colour classes of equal size, constructs a connected tree using only cross-class edges, turns its edge-incidence columns into integer blocks, adds an independently sampled common mask to every column, permutes columns and items, and adds a common high offset. Every edge contributes once to each planted bin, so the certificate is known before the integers exist. The offset makes the values satisfy the paper’s exact bounded interval. No generated instance is solved.
+Section 2.3 and Appendix A define Grouped (k)-way Partition: every bin must take exactly (s) items from each size-(ks) group, and all loads must equal the average. This generator uses the native (k=2,s=1) regime. Theorem 2.2 gives a SETH lower bound for the worst case, but it says nothing about this inverse-generated distribution, so Track A would be unjustified.
 
-Theorem 1.1 is a worst-case ETH lower bound, not an average-case theorem for this distribution, so it does not justify Track A. The paper also explicitly gives the easy mechanisms: Section 1.1 records the (O(nT)) subset-sum dynamic program, a newer \(\widetilde O(n+T)\) algorithm, and exponential-in-(n) exact algorithms; Section 9 stresses the known (O((2-\varepsilon_k)^n)) breakthrough for fixed (k). Lemma 8.14 also notes that the bounded regime (W<n^{10}) is polynomial-time. The generator uses (W\gg n^{10}), but openly remains algorithmic.
+Generation composes identities rather than solving an instance. Each four-group block receives half-differences with high parts (u,u+a,u+b,u+a+b) and a common low tag. The smallest plus largest equal the two middle values. Random block orientation, centres, item order, and global group order hide the carried witness. Finally, (W=n^{10}\cdot\text{max_deficit}) puts every item in the paper’s exact inclusive interval ([W(1-1/n^{10}),W]).
 
-The measured Track B reference is exact balanced meet-in-the-middle subset sum, running in (O(2^{n/2})) time and space. At shipping (n=38), eight seeds averaged 668,750 visited states, 2,295,171 counted operations, and 4.158242 seconds (maximum 5.400438 seconds), solving 8/8. That is routine for software and out of reach by hand. The compact route groups the decimal strings into equal-width blocks, removes each column’s common baseline, and propagates the resulting two-endpoint constraints. Its conservative budget is 187 exact operations.
+The paper also identifies the easy mechanism: Section 2.3 explicitly gives a pseudopolynomial (n^{O(1)}W^{k-1}) dynamic program. The measured reference implementation first cancels group centres and then runs exact word-parallel subset-sum DP in (O(qD/\text{word size})) time. At shipping it solved 8/8 seeds, using at most 33,300,648 counted 64-bit word operations; the eight runs took 2.671 seconds total (0.670 seconds maximum). Software can do that routinely, but a no-tool solver cannot. The compact route notices the quartet decomposition and uses 180 exact arithmetic operations; its executable check also solved 8/8.
 
